38615	{"artifactId":"DEF-613abdb351c2c738","entry":{"id":"migrated-DEF-613abdb351c2c738-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-14T08:14:32.334Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"define","stage":"define","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"p1","ref":"c8","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts:244 bumps a generation so the previous turn's loop stops reporting, and :361-366 keeps reading a superseded/closed turn's output without reporting it so its CLI never stalls.","evidence":"Both anchors resolve to the behaviour the premise describes. The read of vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts:244 returns \"// and the generation bump stops the previous loop reporting. The previous turn's process is\", which matches the claim that a generation bump stops the previous turn's loop from reporting. The read of vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts:361 returns \"// Superseded/closed: report nothing more, but keep reading so the superseded turn's CLI\". The grep /Superseded/closed/ finds that same line, plus the DEF.md:187 citation that quotes it word for word. This matches the claim that a superseded or closed turn's output is still read but not reported, so its CLI does not stall. Only line 361 of the cited 361-366 range was read, and it is the opening comment of the block. The comment states the behaviour; the evidence does not show the code that implements it. The /generation/ grep was truncated at its cap, but the direct reads are enough to confirm both anchors.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Both cited anchors resolve to the exact comments the premise paraphrases, so no correction is needed."}},{"claimId":"p2","ref":"c9","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts:22 declares `export const DEFAULT_TURN_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS = 600_000;` (a 10-minute wait).","evidence":"The read of vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts:22 returned FOUND: `export const DEFAULT_TURN_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS = 600_000;`. A grep for /DEFAULT_TURN_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS\\s*=\\s*600_000/ matched the same source line, plus the DEF.md:188 citation of it. 600_000 ms is 10 minutes, so the \"10-minute wait\" description is correct. The citation resolves to the stated entity at the stated line with the stated value.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Premise verified exactly against the source; no edit needed."}},{"claimId":"p3","ref":"c14","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts:217 acquires the turn lock keyed by chat session id (`deps.locks.acquire(s.id, {`), so the lock is per chat session, not per repository.","evidence":"The read of vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts:217 returned FOUND: `lease = await deps.locks.acquire(s.id, {`. This matches the cited anchor and the quote in DEF.md:193 (c14). It is the only non-test call site of `locks.acquire(` among the 40 grep matches. The lock's test file is vscode-plugin/src/chat/__tests__/session-lock.test.ts, and every acquire there uses session-style keys ('s1', 's2', 's3'). It also checks that different keys don't block each other (e.g. lines 63–64: a second `s1` acquire waits while the `s2` acquire is independent). So the lock is keyed per session. The evidence doesn't show where `s` is declared, so \"s = chat session\" rests on the variable name, the session-lock file and test naming, and the chat/ module location. Nothing here suggests a repository-scoped key.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The citation resolves exactly to the quoted line. The lock is keyed by the session object's id, not by a repository, which matches the premise. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p4","ref":"c10","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts:177 declares an in-memory `waitingExternal` array and :697-712 drains it via `drainExternal` with `waitingExternal.shift()`, dropping still-waiting messages when the panel is gone.","evidence":"Every part of the premise matches the evidence. vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts:177 reads `const waitingExternal: { readonly text: string }[] = [];`, which is the in-memory array. :697 reads `const drainExternal = (): void => {`. :698 returns early when `waitingExternal.length === 0`, and :701 takes the next message with `const next = waitingExternal.shift()!`. :706 is the comment `/** The panel is gone: the messages still waiting are dropped, and the log says how many. */`. In the same block, :710 reads `const n = waitingExternal.length` and :712 clears the array with `waitingExternal.length = 0`, which drops the waiting messages as the premise says. One detail: the cited range :697-712 covers two neighbouring blocks. drainExternal runs from :697 to :704, and the panel-disposal drop runs from :706 to :712. DEF.md:189 already cites them that way (\":697-704 and :706-712\"), so the concept and line numbers are right.","action":"None needed; the premise checks out against the source.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Every cited line resolves to the entity the premise describes: the array at :177, drainExternal starting at :697 with shift() at :701, and the drop on panel disposal at :706-712. Nothing needs changing."}},{"claimId":"p5","ref":"c12","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"src/shared/paths.ts:4 and vscode-plugin/src/daemon/paths.ts:26 both resolve the insrc root as `join(homedir(), '.insrc')`, so same-machine same-user extension hosts share one ~/.insrc.","evidence":"Half of the citation checks out. `src/shared/paths.ts:4` reads `const INSRC_DIR = join(homedir(), '.insrc');`. The other anchor does not say what the premise claims. `vscode-plugin/src/daemon/paths.ts:26` reads `return override !== undefined && override !== '' ? override : join(homedir(), '.insrc', 'daemon');`, with the doc comment at :23 \"The installed-daemon root, honoring INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT like daemon-ctl.sh does\". So the plugin works out the installed-daemon root (`~/.insrc/daemon`, or `$INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT`), not the insrc root. Only 2 places match the pattern `join(homedir(), '.insrc')`: shared/paths.ts:4 and the DEF's own c12 line (DEF.md:191). The vscode-plugin file is not one of them. The same override shows up in `scripts/daemon-ctl.sh:46` (`DAEMON_ROOT=\"${INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT:-$HOME/.insrc/daemon}\"`) and `src/cli/services/maintenance.ts:37`. In both, INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT moves only the daemon checkout. It never feeds INSRC_DIR in shared/paths.ts:4. The conclusion itself still holds: the daemon's shared data directory is the hardcoded `join(homedir(), '.insrc')`, so same-machine, same-user extension hosts reach one ~/.insrc. This is a wrong anchor that still points at the right idea, not a defect that would break the build. One related wording problem: DEF.md:86 says \"without an INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT override\". The evidence shows that override does not relocate ~/.insrc, so the condition is imprecise.","action":"Fix c12. Cite src/shared/paths.ts:4 as the only place that sets ~/.insrc (the daemon's data directory). Describe vscode-plugin/src/daemon/paths.ts:26 correctly, or drop it: it resolves the installed-daemon root, `$INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT ?? join(homedir(), '.insrc', 'daemon')`. Optionally reword DEF.md:86. INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT only moves the daemon checkout, not ~/.insrc, so it should not be listed as a condition for sharing.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The second anchor points to a different value: the daemon install root, not the insrc root. The sharing conclusion rests on src/shared/paths.ts:4 alone, which the evidence confirms. Rewording the citation is safe. Whether to also drop the INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT condition at DEF.md:86 is a wording call for a human.","artifactEdits":[{"find":"`src/shared/paths.ts:4 and vscode-plugin/src/daemon/paths.ts:26` — \"join(homedir(), '.insrc')\"","replace":"`src/shared/paths.ts:4` — \"const INSRC_DIR = join(homedir(), '.insrc');\" (vscode-plugin/src/daemon/paths.ts:26 resolves only the installed-daemon root: $INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT ?? join(homedir(), '.insrc', 'daemon'))"}],"options":["Re-cite c12 to src/shared/paths.ts:4 only (INSRC_DIR = join(homedir(), '.insrc'), the daemon's data dir) and remove the vscode-plugin anchor.","Keep both anchors but describe vscode-plugin/src/daemon/paths.ts:26 correctly: it resolves the installed-daemon root as $INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT ?? join(homedir(), '.insrc', 'daemon').","Do either of the above, and also reword DEF.md:86 to drop 'without an INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT override'. That override moves only the daemon checkout (daemon-ctl.sh:46, maintenance.ts:37), not ~/.insrc."]}},{"claimId":"p6","ref":"c13","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/stream-events.ts:33 and :51 define turn-event kinds 'tool-call' and 'file-edit'.","evidence":"Both line reads came back FOUND: vscode-plugin/src/chat/stream-events.ts:33 reads `readonly kind: 'tool-call';` and vscode-plugin/src/chat/stream-events.ts:51 reads `readonly kind: 'file-edit';`. The repo-wide greps support this. Across the source tree, the only declarations of these kinds are at stream-events.ts:33 and :51. Producers in cli-adapter.ts emit them: 'tool-call' at :617 and :757, and 'file-edit' at :605 and :747. The tests (markers.test.ts, stream-events.test.ts, chat-panel.test.ts, render-registry.test.ts) use both kinds as TurnEvent members.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Both cited lines exactly match the premise, and the kinds are TurnEvent union members that cli-adapter.ts emits. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p7","ref":"c13","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/cli-adapter.ts maps CLI tool_use, tool_call and command items into tool-call and file-edit turn events as the turn runs, and a tool-call event carries its command — so whether a turn has acted is observable before it fails.","evidence":"The gathered evidence supports the premise.\n- **Codex items:** vscode-plugin/src/chat/cli-adapter.ts:749 handles codex items with `if (itemType === 'command_execution' || itemType === 'tool_call')`, so both item types reach the tool-call path.\n- **File edits:** cli-adapter.ts:605 emits `{ kind: 'file-edit', turnId, path: input['file_path'] ... }` from claude tool_use input. cli-adapter.ts:747 emits one codex 'file-edit' per path.\n- **Event types:** stream-events.ts:51 declares `readonly kind: 'file-edit';`, and 'tool-call' is a declared TurnEvent kind (stream-events.ts:192 and the 'tool-call' test fixtures).\n- **Command on the event:** Passing tests confirm it: \"claude: a Bash tool_use sets ToolCallEvent.command from input.command\" and \"claude: tool_use maps to tool-call (with mcp parse) and file-edit with a hunk diff\". Test fixtures carry `{ kind: 'tool-call', ..., command: 'npm test' }` (chat-panel.test.ts:428).\n- **As the turn runs:** The mapping is per-line inside mapLine, while the turn is running. chat-panel.ts:1351/1356 reacts to 'file-edit' and 'tool-result' events mid-turn, so the host can see that a turn has acted before it ends.\n- **One caveat:** `command` is optional. \"claude: a command-less tool_use omits command\" and \"codex: a tool_call item without a command omits command (k2)\" show that some tool-call events have no command. This does not undercut the premise: the tool-call or file-edit event itself is enough to show the turn acted.","action":"None needed; the evidence confirms the premise. Optional wording fix: say the tool-call event carries its command \"when the tool has one\", because command-less tools omit `command`.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise. The only imprecision is that `command` is optional on ToolCallEvent. That does not change the design, because the event's presence alone shows the turn has acted.","options":["Leave as-is","Reword to 'a tool-call event carries its command when the tool has one'"]}},{"claimId":"p8","ref":"c15","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts declares `export function isTransientCliError(message: string): boolean` (cited at :554 in references but :558 in the Assumptions — the two line anchors must agree).","evidence":"The grep finds the declaration at `src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:554:export function isTransientCliError(message: string): boolean {`, and reading line 554 confirms it. Line 558 is the function's body: `return /connection closed mid-response|api error|overloaded|rate.?limit|...|\\b(429|500|502|503|504|529)\\b/i.test(message);`. DEF.md:194 cites `[[c15]]` as `src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:558` but quotes the declaration text that actually sits on line 554. So the :554 anchor is correct and the :558 anchor is stale. It still lands inside the same function, so the concept is right and nothing built from it changes. The function exists, is exported, and has the stated signature.","action":"Change the c15 citation in DEF.md from `cli-provider.ts:558` to `cli-provider.ts:554` so both anchors point at the declaration line. Also update any Assumptions entry that says :558.","fixability":"auto","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Ripgrep and the line read both show the declaration at line 554. Line 558 is the regex return inside the same function, so the :558 anchor is stale. This is a mechanical anchor fix taken from the evidence.","artifactEdits":[{"find":"`src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:558` — \"export function isTransientCliError(message: string): boolean {\"","replace":"`src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:554` — \"export function isTransientCliError(message: string): boolean {\""}]}},{"claimId":"p9","ref":"assumption-4","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The chat plugin keeps no failure classification today; transient-failure classification exists only in the daemon's isTransientCliError.","evidence":"The daemon half of the premise holds. `src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:558` defines `isTransientCliError` with the regex `/connection closed mid-response|api error|overloaded|rate.?limit|too many requests|internal server error|service unavailable|timeout|\\b(429|500|502|503|504|529)\\b/i`. `cli-provider.ts:203` uses it in the retry loop (`if (attempt >= maxAttempts || !isTransientCliError(transienceText(err))) throw err;`), and it is tested in `src/agent/providers/__tests__/cli-failure-message.test.ts:98-163` and `structured-output-root-union.test.ts:259`.\n\nThe other half, that the chat plugin has no failure classification, is a claim that something is absent, and this evidence cannot prove it. None of the three greps returned a match under `vscode-plugin/src/chat`. However, all three hit the 50-match cap and were cut off. The matches come back in path order (docs/ → package-lock.json → src/), and `vscode-plugin/` sorts after `src/`. So the cut-off ends exactly where any plugin match would appear. The last `isTransientCliError` hit shown is `src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:203`, and the `rate.?limit|overloaded` list ends inside `src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:558`. A classifier in the chat plugin, such as a regex on 'rate limit' or 'overloaded' in the plugin's turn or error handling, would fall in the part that was cut. The only plugin path in the evidence is `vscode-plugin/src/delivery/__tests__/delivery-client.test.ts`, and it shows up only as text inside a docs file, not as a match in the plugin. No probe was limited to `vscode-plugin/src/chat`, so that anchor was never checked.","action":"Re-run the absence check limited to `vscode-plugin/src/chat`, with no match cap, for example `rg -n -i 'transient|retry|rate.?limit|overloaded|too many requests|timeout|\\b(429|5\\d\\d)\\b|classif' vscode-plugin/src/chat`. If nothing comes back, the premise holds as written. If the chat plugin already sorts errors into kinds (for example in its CLI adapter or turn-error path), S004's design should name that existing logic and either reuse it or merge it with `isTransientCliError` instead of treating the daemon function as the only starting point.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The daemon-side citation (cli-provider.ts:558) is confirmed, so the anchor needs no change. The claim that the chat plugin has nothing is unproven only because the greps were cut off before reaching vscode-plugin/. The outcome of a limited grep decides which wording is right, so no text edit can be derived from the current evidence.","options":["Keep the premise as written after a grep limited to vscode-plugin/src/chat with no match cap comes back empty. Record that grep as the premise's evidence.","If the limited grep finds error or retry classification in the chat plugin, change the premise to name that location and say S004 must reconcile it with isTransientCliError.","Weaken the premise to 'the chat plugin has no transient-failure classification that we know of; to be confirmed during S004 design', and add an explicit check task to S004."]}},{"claimId":"p10","ref":"c3","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/session-lock.ts detects whether a lock holder's CLI process is alive or gone by its process id.","evidence":"The grep for /process\\.kill\\(.*,\\s*0\\)/ matched `vscode-plugin/src/chat/session-lock.ts:294: process.kill(pid, 0);`. That is a signal-0 liveness probe keyed on a process id, inside the anchored file. The same idiom is used for \"is this pid alive\" checks across the repo, for example `src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts:32` (`try { process.kill(pid, 0); return true; } catch { return false; }`) and `src/daemon/lifecycle.ts:51` (\"signal 0: probe only\"). So session-lock.ts does decide whether a process is alive or gone from its pid. The evidence is a single line, so it does not show that this pid is specifically the lock holder's CLI process. That detail is plausible given the file's name and purpose, and it does not change what gets built.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchored file contains the pid liveness probe process.kill(pid, 0) at line 294, which confirms the premise. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p11","ref":"k1","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"No plugin source (vscode-plugin/src) imports LMDB or LanceDB; the plugin reaches the daemon only over IPC.","evidence":"The repo-wide import probes found no matches under vscode-plugin/src. All 5 `from 'lmdb'` matches are in the daemon's own tree or in docs: src/db/graph/store.ts:56, src/db/__tests__/lmdb-smoke.test.ts:16, src/db/graph/__tests__/helpers/store-child.ts:29-30, and docs/plans/graph-storage-lmdb.md:907. All 43 `@lancedb/lancedb` matches are in docs, package.json:29, package-lock.json, src/db/__tests__/lancedb-smoke.test.ts:17, or src/db/lance/*.ts (conn.ts:26, entity-vec.ts:41, session-vec.ts:17, turn-vec.ts:19, artifact-vec.ts:26, config-vec.ts:20, response-segment-vec.ts:35, working-memory-bullets.ts:32). That list is complete, not truncated, and none of it is in vscode-plugin/src. The `graph\\.lmdb` probe stopped at its 50-match cap, but it searches for a path string, not an import. Its visible hits are docs and design files, and the only plugin-related one is the convention itself (docs/epics/per-repo-chat-turn-queue-outbox-E20261014613abdb3/DEF.md:94). So both import probes show the claimed absence. Two parts are not shown directly: that vscode-plugin/src exists as a tree that was searched, and the positive half of the claim, that the plugin uses IPC only. No evidence contradicts either.","action":"none — verified sound. The plugin imports neither store library. Optional: add a probe that lists vscode-plugin/src to confirm the tree exists, and one that greps it for the daemon socket or IPC client, to back the \"IPC only\" half directly.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise holds against the evidence, so the artifact needs no edit. The only gap is that the IPC-only half rests on absence of evidence. That is not enough to rate it MED, because the prescribed constraint (the chat queue is the plugin's own store, apart from LMDB and Lance) is unchanged either way."}},{"claimId":"p12","ref":"k4","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The turn lifecycle (vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts) does not import the vscode module and receives its dependencies by injection (deps).","evidence":"The first half of the premise holds. The grep for /from 'vscode'/ found exactly one runtime import under vscode-plugin/src: `vscode-plugin/src/extension.ts:13 import * as vscode from 'vscode';`. Every other match is a test assertion that some module does NOT import vscode (activation.test.ts:110, extension-chat-wiring.test.ts:53, exec-path-scan.test.ts:47-48, review-wiring.test.ts:135, onboarding activation.test.ts:48) or a docs/epics line repeating the rule that extension.ts is the only importer (S004/S005/S007 LLD cl7/cl8/cl9). turn-lifecycle.ts does not appear among the matches, so it does not import 'vscode'. The second half has no direct evidence. The /deps\\./ grep was cut off at 50 matches, and none of the shown matches are in vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts. Nothing read from that file shows that it exists, what it exports, or a `deps` parameter. So \"receives its dependencies by injection (deps)\" cannot be checked. The absence of an import is consistent with the project's injectable-boundary convention, but it does not prove the file uses a deps shape.","action":"Confirm that vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts exists. Read its exported factory or function signature to confirm it takes a `deps` (or similarly named) injected parameter, and cite the `file:line` of that signature in the artifact. Also check that the file is covered by a no-'vscode' source-scan test like the one at extension-chat-wiring.test.ts:53. If it is not, add one so the vscode-free claim is enforced and not just observed.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The no-vscode-import claim is supported, because extension.ts:13 is the only 'vscode' importer in vscode-plugin/src. The deps-injection half and the file's existence are not covered by the evidence, which keeps this at MED. The artifact needs an anchor the reviewer can check before the premise can be marked LOW.","options":["Read turn-lifecycle.ts, then cite the signature line that takes `deps` (for example `turn-lifecycle.ts:<n> export function create…(deps: …)`) as the anchor for k4.","Reduce the premise to what the evidence supports: 'turn-lifecycle.ts does not import vscode (extension.ts:13 is the sole importer)'. Keep deps injection as a design intent the build must satisfy, not a fact about existing code.","If turn-lifecycle.ts is new in this Story, reword k4 as a requirement ('MUST NOT import vscode; MUST take injected deps') and add a source-scan test for it, following extension-chat-wiring.test.ts:53."]}},{"claimId":"p13","ref":"c11","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"node:sqlite (DatabaseSync) loads unflagged in the VS Code 1.141 extension host (Electron 43.7.7, Node 24.21.0); the plugin's minimum VS Code engine must be raised to such a version.","evidence":"The second half of the premise is confirmed: vscode-plugin/package.json:23 declares `\"vscode\": \"^1.75.0\"`, mirrored in vscode-plugin/package-lock.json:18. The floor is far below 1.141, so it does have to be raised.\n\nThe first half, that node:sqlite loads unflagged in the 1.141 extension host, has no source evidence. The only support is the stakeholder citation at DEF.md:190: a smoke check run on 2026-10-14 with `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 /usr/share/code/code -e \"...require('node:sqlite').DatabaseSync...\"`, which printed `24.21.0 43.7.7 'node:sqlite OK'`. The engine did not re-run that check.\n\nThat check runs Electron's binary as plain Node. It does not exercise the real extension host (a utility process with its own flags and environment), so the claim that it \"loads in the extension host\" is inferred, not observed. The node:sqlite hits under src/ (for example src/daemon/db/drivers/sqlite.ts:13) are daemon code running under the daemon's own Node 22/24. They say nothing about the plugin host.\n\nNothing names the exact floor version either. DEF.md:85 and k5 defer the floor to the design (\"a verified version\"). This premise names 1.141 only as the version that was tested, not as the minimum where the module first works. So the claim cannot be verified from this evidence. The prescribed change (raise engines.vscode, and show a plain error when the module cannot load) still holds whatever the exact floor turns out to be.","action":"1. Check the claim in the real extension host, not only with ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE. A minimal extension, or a startup probe in the plugin, should `require('node:sqlite')` and open a `:memory:` DatabaseSync in VS Code 1.141.\n2. Have the design pin the exact `engines.vscode` floor. Either use `^1.141.0` (the version actually tested) or find the earliest VS Code whose Electron Node exposes node:sqlite without a flag. Update vscode-plugin/package.json:23 and the lockfile to match.\n3. Keep k5's startup fallback (a plain \"VS Code version unsupported\" message when the require throws), so a host without the module fails visibly.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Package.json evidence confirms the floor must be raised, but the node:sqlite-in-extension-host claim rests only on a stakeholder ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE smoke test, not on the extension host itself, and the exact floor is not pinned. Rewording the premise or adding a verification step needs human sign-off.","options":["Pin engines.vscode to ^1.141.0 (the version actually tested) and record that the smoke test was ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE, with an extension-host probe as a build acceptance check.","Find the earliest VS Code release whose bundled Electron/Node exposes node:sqlite without a flag, verify it in a real extension host, and use that as the floor.","Keep the premise as written but downgrade it to 'verified under ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE only'. Rely on the k5 runtime fallback message to cover any extension-host difference."]}},{"claimId":"p14","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The Epic has exactly 8 epic-level FRs (FR001-FR008) and 5 stories (S001-S005), each with 4 story FRs.","evidence":"The grep for `E20261014613abdb3:FR00[0-9]` returns exactly 8 epic-level FRs, FR001 through FR008, at DEF.md:28-35. The grep for `E20261014613abdb3:S00[1-5]:FR00[1-4]` returns 20 matches. They split evenly at 4 FRs per story: S001 at DEF.md:39-42, S002 at :46-49, S003 at :53-56, S004 at :60-63 and S005 at :67-70. That makes 5 stories with 4 FRs each, which matches the premise exactly.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the count: 8 epic FRs, 5 stories and 4 FRs per story. No change is needed."}},{"claimId":"p15","ref":"k2","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"Stakeholder constraint k2 fixes: 10-minute heartbeat lease takeover requiring process gone; 3 infrastructure retries at 10 s, 1 min, 5 min only before the turn acted; ~500 records per repository with finished removed first and overflow refused — and FR005, FR006, FR008, S003 and S004 restate these same values consistently.","evidence":"This premise is only partly verified. The evidence checks the lease values but not the retry or retention values.\n\nVerified (lease takeover):\n- DEF.md:95 (k2) says \"a lease taken over only after 10 minutes without a heartbeat and with the turn's process gone\". It also lists \"infrastructure retries limited to 3 (10 s, 1 min, 5 min) and only before the turn acted\" and \"about 500 records per repository, finished ones removed first, an overflowing message refused with a note\".\n- DEF.md:32 (FR005) says \"only after 10 minutes without a sign of life from it and once its process is confirmed gone\".\n- DEF.md:53 (S003:FR001) says \"only after 10 minutes without that sign of life and once the turn's process is confirmed gone\". This matches k2.\n- DEF.md:142-143 (ac1/ac2) and SPEC.md:15 give the same 10-minute value plus the process-gone condition.\n\nNot verified:\n- FR006, FR008 and S004:FR001 do not appear in any match. The /500/ grep was truncated at 50 matches, and none of those matches are in the per-repo-chat-turn-queue DEF.\n- The retry values (3 retries at 10 s / 1 min / 5 min) and the ~500-record cap only appear in k2 itself.\n\nSo the claim that \"FR006, FR008 and S004 restate these same values consistently\" cannot be checked from this evidence. Nothing gathered contradicts it.","action":"Re-run the probes scoped to docs/epics/per-repo-chat-turn-queue-outbox-E20261014613abdb3/DEF.md, grepping for \"10 s\", \"5 min\" and \"500\". Read the lines for FR006, FR008 and S004:FR001 and confirm each one states the same retry schedule (3 retries at 10 s/1 min/5 min, only before the turn acted) and the same retention rule (~500 per repo, finished ones removed first, overflow refused) as k2. If any of them differs, align it to k2.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The lease half of the premise is confirmed by the k2, FR005 and S003:FR001 lines. The retry and retention halves, and the FR006, FR008 and S004:FR001 anchors, were never reached because the /500/ grep was truncated before this DEF. The text is probably right, but it needs a scoped re-check before it counts as verified.","options":["Re-probe the DEF with scoped greps for '10 s|1 min|5 min' and '500' and confirm that FR006, FR008 and S004:FR001 match k2.","Narrow the premise to the verified part (the lease values in k2, FR005 and S003:FR001) and file the retry and retention consistency as a separate premise with its own anchors.","Accept with a note that the FR006, FR008 and S004:FR001 consistency was not verified by the engine."]}},{"claimId":"p16","kind":"ordering","severity":"MED","premise":"Story dependencies: S002 and S003 depend on S001; S004 and S005 depend on S001 and S002; S001 has no dependencies.","evidence":"The grep for /Depends on/ hit its 50-match cap and spans several unrelated epics. It never says which epic's DEF this premise belongs to. None of the dependency graphs it shows match the premise. (1) add-bugfix-triage-category DEF.md:57 and :70 give two stories `s1` as their dependency, which fits S002 and S003 depending on S001. But DEF.md:90 and :104 give `s2`, `s3` as the dependencies of the next two stories, not `s1`, `s2` as the premise says for S004 and S005. (2) add-build-workflow DEF.md:71/92/115/129 is a chain: s1 → s2 → s3 → (s3, s4). (3) add-daemon-driven-code-review DEF.md:79/100/122/143 makes four stories depend on `s1`. DEF.md:158 makes one depend on `s2`, `s3`, `s4`, `s5`, and DEF.md:180/195 make two depend on `s6`. Because the results were truncated, the target epic's DEF may not be in the evidence at all. Nothing gathered shows S004 or S005 depending on exactly S001 and S002, and nothing shows that S001 has no dependencies.","action":"Re-run the probe on the target epic's DEF.md only, and compare the premise with each story's `**Depends on:**` line. If the target is add-bugfix-triage-category-insrc-framework-E202609181703991c, fix the premise: S004 and S005 depend on `s2` and `s3` (DEF.md:90 and :104), not on S001 and S002. Also check that any build ordering based on this premise still puts S003 before S004 and S005.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence was truncated and covers several epics, so the premise can't be checked against one artifact. The closest match is the bugfix-triage epic. There, S002 and S003 depend on s1 as the premise says, but S004 and S005 depend on s2 and s3, not on s1 and s2. Without knowing for sure which artifact is the target, a find/replace edit isn't safe.","options":["If the target is the bugfix-triage epic, change the premise to: 'S002 and S003 depend on S001; S004 and S005 depend on S002 and S003; S001 has no dependencies.'","Re-probe with a path scoped to the target epic's DEF.md, then re-judge the premise.","Keep the premise only if the target epic's DEF.md is found to list `s1`, `s2` for S004 and S005."]}},{"claimId":"p17","ref":"c1","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"The Define is seeded from approved spec SPEC-6932e6aabd83765d (approved 2026-10-14), which is the source of constraints k2 and k5.","evidence":"The evidence supports the premise. DEF.md:8 reads \"**Seeded from:** `SPEC-6932e6aabd83765d`\", and DEF.md:180 has the citation \"**[[c1]]** `prior-artifact` `SPEC-6932e6aabd83765d (approved 2026-10-14)`\". That matches the premise's anchor and its approval date. The cited artifact exists: docs/standalone/per-repo-chat-turn-queue-outbox-E202610146932e6aa/SPEC.md:1 contains \"<!-- insrc:artifact SPEC-6932e6aabd83765d -->\". The evidence does not show the approval stamp inside SPEC.md, and it does not show that k2 and k5 come from this spec. Those two points rely only on the DEF's own c1 citation. Neither one changes what gets built.","action":"None needed; the premise is verified. Optional: open SPEC.md and confirm it is marked approved and that it states the k2 and k5 constraints.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Verified: the spec ID resolves to a real SPEC artifact, and the DEF's seed line and its c1 citation both match the premise. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p18","ref":"problem","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Only the newest turn reports to the chat panel; a superseded turn keeps running its CLI out of sight while the new message waits behind the per-session lock.","evidence":"The evidence supports all three parts of the premise. (1) Only the newest turn reports: vscode-plugin/src/chat/turn-lifecycle.ts:244 reads \"and the generation bump stops the previous loop reporting.\" A newer turn advances the generation, so the older turn's reporting loop goes quiet. (2) The new message waits behind the per-session lock: turn-lifecycle.ts:217 reads \"lease = await deps.locks.acquire(s.id, {\". The lock is per session id and is awaited. session-lock.test.ts:58-64 confirms that a second acquire on the same id waits (onWaiting) while a different id ('s2') does not. The defect's own LLD (docs/standalone/defect-vscode-plugin-src-chat-chat-E202610108ca7382b/S001/LLD.md:71) adds that the lease is released by the attached process's exit. So the new turn can't proceed until the old CLI process exits. The DEF of the target Epic cites the same anchor (DEF.md:193 [[c14]] turn-lifecycle.ts:217). (3) The superseded turn keeps running its CLI: line 244 goes on with \"The previous turn's process is…\". The read is cut off at that point, but the sentence is clearly about the old process not being stopped. That fits a turn whose reporting is silenced while it still holds the lease. One small gap: the rest of line 244 isn't in the evidence, so the literal wording \"keeps running\" isn't quoted. The meaning is clear from the generation-bump comment and the lease-release-on-process-exit design. The anchor in chat-panel.ts wasn't probed, but the premise doesn't depend on it.","action":"None needed; the premise is verified. Optionally read turn-lifecycle.ts:244-246 to quote the full sentence about the previous turn's process.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise, so no edit to the artifact is needed."}},{"claimId":"p19","ref":"s5/ac3","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Message images/file attachments already live in a plugin attachment store that clears old files on start-up, and messages reference them by path.","evidence":"The gathered evidence supports the premise, though it comes from the design docs and build record of the earlier paste-images Story. None of it is a direct read of the source file.\n\n- **The store exists.** docs/standalone/paste-images-attach-documents-dev-chat-E2026101259da3204/S001/LLD.md:79 declares the new `vscode-plugin/src/chat/attachment-store.ts` with `interface AttachmentRef { ... readonly path: string }`. S001/CR.md:26 then reviews `vscode-plugin/src/chat/attachment-store.ts:1`, which shows the file was built.\n- **Old files are cleared on start-up.** LLD.md:112 says extension.ts \"sweeps at start with the same keep set and age as the output sweep\". It also says the store's onEvict removes a session's attachments.\n- **Messages refer to files by path.** BUILD.md:11 says attachments are \"copied to the chat's own folder under ~/.insrc/chat-attachments ... sent with the next message as paths\". LLD.md:118 says the stored user row gains `attachments?: readonly AttachmentRef[]`, and each ref carries `path`.\n- **The new Epic says the same.** The Epic's DEF.md:80 (\"They already live in the attachment store; a message refers to them by path\") and DEF.md:176 (ac3) match this.\n\nOne detail matters: the start-up sweep is not a plain \"clear old files\". It works from a keep set plus an age limit (LLD.md:112), and session eviction removes attachments too. So a queued message's image survives only if its session is in the keep set or the file is newer than the age limit. This does not change what s5/ac3 asks for. It does tell the build where to hook in.","action":"None needed; the premise checks out. Recommended: when building s5/ac3, add the attachment paths of queued and outbox messages to the start-up sweep's keep set, or exempt them from the age limit. Also make sure onEvict does not delete files that a queued message still refers to. Before relying on the sweep's exact signature, read vscode-plugin/src/chat/attachment-store.ts and its sweep call in extension.ts.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise is confirmed by the paste-images LLD (:79, :112, :118), its BUILD record (:11) and its code review, which cites attachment-store.ts. No edit to the artifact is needed. The only gap is how precise the wording is: the sweep works from a keep set plus an age limit rather than blanket clearing, and that tells the build where to protect queued images."}}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":7,"low":12},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-14T08:14:32.334Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
